1: # P3K.24. The actual reduced-III page and positive parameter return
2: 
3: Status: proved. The physical-character arrow, actual page and line
4: maps, and signed Thom comparison have passed root and independent
5: audits. This note continues P3F.37's construction and complete
6: integral topology. The earlier working statuses and origin convention
7: are retained in the dated historical records cited below.
8: 
9: Fix m=4l+3, l>=0, and the source
10: \[
11:  S:\ y^2=x^3+tx+1,\quad G=(0,1),\quad
12:  M=\mathcal O(2G-O),\quad Q=G.
13: \]
14: Use the single reduced-III cap with A_r^m=B_{r^m}, in the marked
15: single-A cut convention, and choose one of the three I1 fibres as
16: the simple whole-fibre zero. The parameter is r, with lambda=r^m.
17: The same argument below also treats the explicit second source
18: \[
19:  S':\ y^2=x^3+tx-t,\quad G=(1,1),
20: \]
21: whose fibres are III* at infinity, II at zero, and I1 at -27/4.
22: The precise root and source-complement extension for this second
23: source is recorded at the end.
24: 
25: ## III-R.1. The exact positive angular clutch
26: 
27: Let t_c be the selected I1 value, q=t-t_c,
28: s=log(q)/(2pi i), and v=log(r)/(2pi i). The actual multiplier
29: of the suspension line in the chosen cusp frame is
30: exp(2pi i C(q)) q r^m. Use the physical source Abel coordinate
31: a=zeta_1 and the physical line character V=exp(2pi i zeta_2).
32: Let g(q) be the holomorphic Tate logarithm of +G. The contracting
33: line map and its inverse act exactly by
34: \[
35:  \begin{aligned}
36:  B(a,V)&=(a-g,\ e^{2\pi i C}q r^m V),\\
37:  B^{-1}(a,V)&=(a+g,\ e^{-2\pi i C}q^{-1}r^{-m}V).
38:  \end{aligned}
39: \]
40: The positive outward suspension period is consequently
41: \[
42:  \gamma_{\rm out}(s,v)=\binom{g(q)}{-C(q)-s-mv},\qquad
43:  \delta=\binom01,
44: \]
45: and it represents B^{-1}, exactly the positive gamma used in the
46: norm-shell meridian and the weighted Seifert page. Delta is the
47: positive physical scalar rotation; neither circle has been silently
48: inverted. The actual ordinary-to-cap cochain square in the
49: construction note makes the positive parameter deck
50: (v,zeta)->(v+1,zeta+s delta). Its inverse closing map multiplies
51: the physical line by (t-t_c)^{-1}. Indeed F_c:ordinary->cap has
52: g_c(s_infty+1)=Dg_c(s_infty)+a_c, hence its scalar change is
53: (c-c_0)s_infty delta/m. The identity cap closing gives
54: F_c^{-1}F_{c+m}=+s_infty delta. Its global multiplier is
55: (t-t_c)^{-1}; the positive deck is the inverse multiplier.
56: 
57: Use the auxiliary cusp cover q=Q_0^{2m}. The two extending origins
58: are
59: \[
60:  o(s,v)=\binom{-g s/m}{Cs/m+s^2/(2m)+sv},\qquad
61:  \sigma(s,v)=\binom{g v}{-Cv-m(v^2-v)/2}.
62: \]
63: Their exact differences are
64: \[
65:  \begin{aligned}
66:  o(s,v+1)-o(s,v)&=s\delta,\\
67:  \sigma(s,v+1)-\sigma(s,v)&=s\delta+\gamma_{\rm out}(s,v),\\
68:  o(s+2m,v)-o(s,v)&=-2\gamma_{\rm out}(s,v)+2m\delta,\\
69:  \sigma(s+2m,v)-\sigma(s,v)&=0.
70:  \end{aligned}                                                   \tag{III-R.1}
71: \]
72: The factor 2 in this auxiliary cusp cover is retained: m is odd,
73: and the difference for s->s+m would contain +m delta/2.
74: The discrepancies in (III-R.1) are actual integral periods.
75: The cusp origin sigma has bounded characters in q and hence extends
76: in the actual unimodular cusp charts. Subtracting o removes the
77: parameter deck on the ordinary collar. For every nonzero q its
78: remaining multiplier is an invertible unit times r^m; the actual
79: m-node parameter chain, with charts X_iY_i=r and
80: X_i=V/r^i, Y_i=r^{i+1}/V, gives the other extending origin.
81: These are two collar comparisons, with no asserted two-variable
82: proper analytic corner.
83: 
84: Their difference is
85: \[
86:  F(s,v)=\binom{g(s/m+v)}
87:  {-C(s/m+v)-(s+mv)^2/(2m)+mv/2}.
88: \]
89: Direct subtraction gives
90: \[
91:  F(s,v+\theta)-F(s,v)
92:    =\theta\gamma_{\rm out}(s,v)-\frac m2\theta(\theta-1)\delta.
93:                                                                \tag{III-R.2}
94: \]
95: The last coefficient is zero at both endpoints. Its exponential is
96: a periodic unitary gauge, homotopic through endpoint-fixed gauges
97: to the constant gauge. It disappears in the delta-circle quotient.
98: Under the retained cusp deck s->s+2m, this angular difference changes
99: by -2m theta delta. Its line transition has winding -2m. Consequently
100: the actual positive r-loop makes one positive gamma turn, with the
101: outward B^{-1} marking verified above. It is not an m-fold positive
102: gamma turn. The earlier inward-character origins and inverse-closing
103: paragraph are retained verbatim in
104: records/pre_gamma_dictionary_20261117T070909Z_part3_reduced_iii_return.md;
105: the complete physical-character square in this section supersedes
106: them. No earlier family's closing arrow is used to select this sign.
107: 
108: ## III-R.2. The marked Seifert space and its page
109: 
110: The ordinary quotient by the source elliptic direction has coordinates
111: (t,gamma). At the cap put q_infty=1/t and use the actual root variable
112: z, q_infty=z^m, and compatible positive norm coordinate u. The
113: single-A cut convention is
114: \[
115:  (z,u)\sim(\zeta_m z,u-1),\qquad
116:  u=m\gamma-\arg(q_\infty)/(2\pi).
117: \]
118: This is precisely the weighted circle fibration on the standard
119: three-sphere with weights (1,m). Here is an explicit map retaining
120: the sign and meridian. Let R(z)>0 be the unique solution of
121: \[
122:  R^2+|z|^2R^{2/m}=1.
123: \]
124: Send (z,u) to
125: \[
126:  (z_1,z_2)=
127:  \bigl(zR^{1/m}e^{2\pi i u/m},\ R e^{2\pi i u}\bigr)\in S^3.
128:                                                                \tag{III-R.3}
129: \]
130: The two factors from (z,u)->(zeta_m z,u-1) cancel exactly.
131: The inverse on the cap is obtained by choosing a real argument of
132: z_2 and then recovering z; a different choice is exactly that
133: equivalence. The base coordinate is t=z_2/z_1^m and the positive
134: gamma action is (z_1,z_2)->(e^{2pi i theta}z_1,
135: e^{2pi i m theta}z_2). Thus q maps to -h and gamma to m h,
136: as in the construction's degree-one gluing matrix.
137: 
138: Choose a regular binding at t=t_* close to, but distinct from,
139: t_c. Its positive Milnor function is z_2-t_*z_1^m. The affine
140: page is the graph
141: \[
142:  b-t_*a^m=1,
143: \]
144: embedded in S^3 by (a,b)->(rho a,rho^m b), where rho>0 is
145: the unique solution of rho^2|a|^2+rho^{2m}|b|^2=1.
146: Its inverse divides by the positive m-th root of the Milnor
147: function on the selected argument page. Its projection is exactly
148: \[
149:                          t=t_*+a^{-m}.                \tag{III-R.4}
150: \]
151: The compactification is P1_a. It is branched to order m at a=0
152: (the additive cap) and a=infinity (the regular binding). The open
153: page C^o is a disc, with one boundary circle at the binding. The
154: chosen cusp and every other finite source critical value are
155: unramified. Each has m preimages. This retains the regular-binding
156: cover; a cover branched instead at the cusp is a different map.
157: 
158: ## III-R.3. The actual cap slice and line on the page
159: 
160: The full cap orbit is the actual mapping torus of a_0 on the
161: resolved root source, in the same norm coordinate u. In (III-R.3),
162: the page equation is
163: \[
164:  2\pi u+\arg(1-t_*z^m)=\varphi\pmod{2\pi}.
165: \]
166: Choose the small cap disc so the displayed argument has a single
167: real branch. Its lifts are the graphs
168: \[
169:  u=u_0(z)+j,\quad
170:  u_0(z)=\varphi/(2\pi)-\arg(1-t_*z^m)/(2\pi),\quad j\in Z.
171: \]
172: The actual A_r map sends one graph to the next while applying
173: a_0 to its source point. Every orbit has a unique representative
174: on j=0: the function u_0 is invariant under z->zeta_m z, so
175: two such representatives differing by A_r^j would satisfy
176: u_0(z)=u_0(z)-j, which forces j=0. Hence the map
177: \[
178:  p\longmapsto[p,u_0(z(p))]
179: \]
180: identifies the entire resolved III source tube with the actual
181: page near the exceptional core. It includes both components and
182: their tangency point. There is no residual stabilizer quotient.
183: The principal delta circle above that graph is the norm sphere
184: of N=O(2G'-O) with a prescribed smooth positive radius. Radial
185: normalization identifies it with the unit circle of that exact
186: line N. In particular its component degrees are zero.
187: 
188: On the other finite pieces the page is the unramified pullback of
189: the ordinary source and line. The fixed-cusp orbit product is the
190: actual P3F.9 comparison relative to the source and gamma boundary
191: marking. The page is chosen disjoint from the fixed sphere, as in
192: P3K.6 with degree m; its m phase values avoid the short compression
193: arc. These maps construct a locally translation-isomorphic affine
194: elliptic fibration over C^o, including the resolved III and the
195: actual nodal fibres. Its Jacobian is the minimal source pullback
196: under (III-R.4).
197: 
198: Here is a comparison that also fixes the actual binding collar.
199: Let Lambda be its elliptic period local system on the complement
200: of the critical values. In one sector of (III-R.4), the three
201: original nodal vanishing paths lift with their original marking.
202: Their vanishing cycles span Lambda integrally, by the trace-mod-d^2
203: calculation in the construction note. Each transvection image is
204: the primitive subgroup generated by its vanishing cycle. Thus the
205: global coinvariant group is zero. Poincare duality with compact
206: supports on the punctured oriented base gives
207: H_c^2(Lambda)=H_0(Lambda)=0. The quotient j_*Lambda/j_!Lambda is
208: a finite skyscraper sheaf and has H^1=0. Its exact sequence then
209: gives
210: \[
211:               H^2(C^o,\partial C^o;j_*\Lambda)=0.       \tag{III-R.5}
212: \]
213: For the source with II+I1, the two vanishing cycles in a morsification
214: of the actual II fibre meet once and already span the lattice, so
215: the same calculation applies. That local morsification is used only
216: to compute the two integral vanishing relations of the cusp fibre.
217: 
218: Use the sheaf of smooth sections of the Neron translation group,
219: whose translations extend to the actual minimal regular source.
220: Its identity part has the local exponential sequence with kernel
221: j_*Lambda and a fine Lie-algebra sheaf. Surjectivity at a reduced
222: III fibre follows from its additive identity group and the analytic
223: inverse-function theorem; at a node it is the corresponding
224: multiplicative exponential. Its component quotient is the
225: skyscraper Z/2 at the III value. Its H^1, also relative to the
226: ordinary binding collar, is zero. Consequently (III-R.5) removes
227: the translation cocycle relative to that collar. More explicitly,
228: remove its component cocycle by local component sections, lift its
229: remaining cocycle to the Lie bundle, remove the integral period
230: two-cocycle by (III-R.5), and solve the resulting smooth Lie cocycle
231: with a partition of unity vanishing on the binding collar. The
232: resulting translations are mutually inverse smooth isomorphisms
233: to the sectioned pullback, equal to the given comparison on that
234: collar. Their extensions across III are the extending source
235: translations already used in the cap.
236: 
237: The line comparison retains more than the generic fibre degree.
238: On the cap it is the actual N just identified. Everywhere else
239: its fibrewise Picard point is 2G', as fixed by the two elliptic
240: period multipliers. Translation acts trivially on this Picard
241: point, including its specialization at III. We use the rigidified
242: relative Picard isomorphism before pulling back by the smooth
243: translation, so that no holomorphic divisor is assigned to a merely
244: smooth total-space map. Concretely, on a holomorphic chart of the
245: extending translation group, let T be the universal source
246: translation and form Hom(N,T^*N) on the fibre product with that
247: chart. On each compact source fibre this line is trivial. For
248: ordinary elliptic fibres this is the degree-zero group law; at
249: the III value N itself is trivial (the explicit doubling below
250: puts 2G' at O), and at a node the normalization's multiplicative
